 "result": "I couldn't get the answer. Every query failed with \"database is locked,\" across about 12 tries. Even `SELECT 1`, which doesn't touch any table, failed every time, so the problem is the database connection, not my query. I stopped retrying because the lock never cleared, and more tries would just keep using budget for the same error.\n\nI did get the table layouts before the lock started:\n- `orders`: `id`, `customer_id`, `item`, `total_eur`, `ordered_on` (stored as text)\n- `customers`: `id`, `name`, `city`\n\nThe lock is probably coming from another process holding a write lock or an open transaction, or from a leftover `-journal`/`-wal` file next to the database. Once that's cleared, check what `ordered_on` looks like with `SELECT ordered_on FROM orders LIMIT 10`, since it's text and the date format matters for comparing. If it's in ISO format (`YYYY-MM-DD...`), this query gives the answer:\n\n```sql\nSELECT c.name, o.ordered_on\nFROM orders o JOIN customers c ON c.id = o.customer_id\nWHERE o.ordered_on < '2026-10-01'\nORDER BY o.ordered_on DESC\nLIMIT 1;\n```\n\nIf the dates use another format, like `DD/MM/YYYY`, the comparison needs rewriting first. When the database is available again, I'll run it and give you the customer name.",
